<p data-start="60986" data-end="62029">\begin{abstract}<br>
Synthetic morphogenesis and collective intelligence are key frontiers in bio-inspired robotics. We introduce the \textbf{Xenobot Swarm Simulator}, an open-source, WebGL-accelerated platform modeling swarms of genetically evolving agents that forage in a dynamic nutrient field ($\Phi$) while depositing and sensing entropy trails ($S$). Agents follow a \textbf{Resource-Seeking Vector Potential (RSVP)} framework combining boids-like flocking, chemotaxis, and stigmergic feedback. Fitness is defined as collected food minus an entropy penalty ($\lambda \cdot \int S$), explicitly selecting for path efficiency. Using t-SNE visualization and large-scale batch simulations, we demonstrate the emergence of \textit{specialized foraging castes}, \textit{adaptive trail avoidance}, and \textit{near-optimal nutrient harvesting}. Results suggest that entropy-regularized evolution in active matter systems can produce scalable, robust collective behaviors analogous to biological morphogenesis and active inference.<br>
\end{abstract}</p>
<p data-start="62033" data-end="62500">\section{Introduction}<br>
The design of autonomous multi-agent systems capable of adaptive, self-organized behavior remains a grand challenge in artificial life and swarm robotics \citep{bonabeau1999swarm}. Recent advances in xenobiology \citep{kriegman2021xenobots} and active inference \citep{friston2010free} suggest that \textit{morphogenetic fields}—spatiotemporal patterns of activity and information—may serve as a unifying substrate for collective intelligence.</p>
<p data-start="62504" data-end="63024">We present the \textbf{Xenobot Swarm Simulator}, a high-performance simulation environment implementing an \textbf{RSVP (Resource-Seeking Vector Potential)} field that integrates: (i) nutrient diffusion ($\Phi$), (ii) entropy trails ($S$), (iii) boids-like flocking with evolvable weights, and (iv) genetic evolution with explicit entropy cost in fitness. Our hypothesis is that \textbf{selection against path redundancy} (via $\lambda \cdot S$) drives \textit{efficient collective foraging} without centralized control.</p>
<p data-start="63028" data-end="63444">\section{Methods}<br>
\subsection{Dynamics}<br>
Nutrient field $\Phi(x,y,t)$ evolves via<br>
\begin{equation}<br>
\Phi_{t+1} = \Phi_t \cdot d + (1-d) \cdot \nabla^2_5(\Phi) + r (1 - \Phi_t),<br>
\end{equation}<br>
where $d \in [0.8,1.0]$ is diffusion and $r \in [0,0.01]$ regrowth.<br>
Entropy trail $S(x,y,t)$ decays exponentially: $S_{t+1}=\delta S_t$, $\delta \in [0.9,1.0]$, and increases on agent visits by $0.02m$ ($m$ metabolism).</p>
<p data-start="63448" data-end="63844">Agent $i$ updates velocity $\mathbf{v}_i$ as<br>
\begin{equation}<br>
\mathbf{v}_i \leftarrow \mathbf{v}<em data-start="63546" data-end="63746">i + \Delta t \left( w_c \mathbf{C} + w_s \mathbf{S} + w_a \mathbf{A} + w_e \mathbf{N} + k \nabla \Phi \right),<br>
\end{equation}<br>
with $|\mathbf{v}<em data-start="63692" data-end="63701">i|\le v</em>{\max}$. Genome: ${w_c, w_s, w_a, w_e, R, v</em>{\max}, m}\in\mathbb{R}^7$. Fitness: $f_i=\text{food}_i - \lambda \int S(\mathbf{x}_i(t),t),dt$.</p>
<p data-start="63848" data-end="64127">\subsection{Visualization and Analysis}<br>
We render fields with WebGL and compute a 2D embedding of genomes using t-SNE (\texttt{tsne-js}, perplexity 30). Dashboards include $\nabla \Phi$, entropy histograms, and genome maps. CSV export provides per-generation and per-agent logs.</p>
